    description: "Named after the Dutch painter — light positioned to create a small triangle of light on the shadow side of the face, a classic portrait technique conveying depth and character. Rembrandt van Rijn developed this lighting naturally in his self-portraits, and Hollywood cinematographers adopted it as the gold standard for dramatic portraiture. Sven Nykvist, Ingmar Bergman's longtime cinematographer, used Rembrandt lighting extensively in \"Fanny and Alexander\" and \"Cries and Whispers.\" Conrad Hall employed it throughout \"Road to Perdition,\" and it remains the go-to lighting pattern for dramatic headshots and interview setups worldwide.",
